# Vendor-neutral observability

`observability.provider: opentelemetry` adds manual OpenTelemetry instrumentation to
the API and the web health client. No collector or commercial account is provisioned.
The generated app never imports the Harness at runtime.

## Local diagnostics

Start with `make up`. Open the frontend and check the API. Browser console events
`web_span` contain the client span/trace IDs. `docker compose logs api` contains the
corresponding server span and `http_request` event. The server's parent ID matches
the web span ID; both have the same trace ID. The Vite same-origin `/api` proxy
preserves `traceparent`. No permissive CORS policy is required.

The two response headers `x-request-id` and `x-trace-id` aid support diagnostics.
An incoming request ID is accepted only when it is 1–64 alphanumeric, hyphen or
underscore characters. Invalid IDs are replaced. Invalid W3C trace parents start
a new trace. These IDs are diagnostic information, never authentication or tenant identity.
Unhandled failures that reach FastAPI's outer error middleware may not carry those
response headers; the request log still records status 500 and its correlation IDs.

Uvicorn uses `app/infrastructure/logging.json`. Request logs contain timestamp,
level, method, registered route template, status, duration and IDs. Arbitrary runtime
messages are represented as `runtime_log`, with logger/severity and exception type
when available. Raw messages, exception text and tracebacks are intentionally omitted;
applications can extend the formatter with reviewed, explicit diagnostic fields.
Bodies, auth headers, query strings, baggage and vendor tracestate are not captured.
Do not place sensitive data in diagnostic request IDs or custom telemetry fields.

## Traces and metrics

The API creates a SERVER span for every sampled HTTP request; the web health helper
creates a CLIENT span. Metrics count requests and record request duration in seconds
using buckets from 5 ms to 10 s. Attributes are method, route template and status.
Unknown paths share `unmatched`; identifiers and query parameters do not become labels.
OpenTelemetry may attach sampled trace exemplars to metrics; these are not metric labels.

`/api/health` retains liveness behavior; `/api/readiness` retains dependency readiness.
Telemetry collector reachability is not a readiness requirement and diagnostics do
not replace dependency checks. There is no public metrics/debug endpoint; metrics
are pushed or logged rather than exposing operational data anonymously.

Server settings (only in the API environment):

| Variable | Behavior |
| --- | --- |
| `TELEMETRY_EXPORTER` | `console` (default), `otlp` or `none` |
| `TELEMETRY_SAMPLE_RATIO` | 0–1 sampling for new API roots; remote sampling is honored |
| `OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT` | Explicit HTTP(S) collector endpoint when using `otlp`, e.g. `http://collector:4318` |
| `OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_HEADERS` | Standard SDK authentication headers; never sent to the browser |

OTLP uses HTTP/protobuf `/v1/traces` and `/v1/metrics`, compatible with standard
OpenTelemetry collectors. Use HTTPS for remote collectors carrying credentials.
The trace exporter batches asynchronously; metrics export every 30 seconds and on
shutdown. Network exports use a three-second timeout. Exporter failure does not make
successful application requests fail, but telemetry may be lost. `none` disables
API exports while correlation and structured logs remain; it does not disable the
frontend's console spans. Browser spans are local console evidence in this phase,
not uploaded to a remote collector. The web helper currently samples every request.

## Scope and verification

This baseline covers web → API. It does not automatically instrument database queries,
AI prompts, application jobs or the API → worker boundary. Add explicit adapters for
those boundaries when needed, with reviewed data capture and context propagation.
No service credentials, request bodies or AI prompt data are exposed to the frontend.

`make test` checks propagation, bounded metric labels, rejection of malformed context,
sanitized failures and real OTLP SDK protobuf encoding using a simulated HTTP session.
The repository's `scripts/smoke_observability.py` runs the actual web helper in Node
through the running Vite proxy and API, then compares exported trace and parent IDs.
It also checks actual JSON logs, shutdown metric export, frontend build and combined
configuration with AI/persistence/jobs/auth. This transport test is not a browser UI E2E test.
